1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,208
Good salary, and still broke by the end of the month?

2
00:00:03,628 --> 00:00:08,435
You're not bad with money. Your spending grew with your pay. It has a name: lifestyle creep.

3
00:00:09,205 --> 00:00:13,246
Here's the math of how a raise disappears, and the one rule that stops it.

4
00:00:14,016 --> 00:00:15,594
First, where the raises went.

5
00:00:16,014 --> 00:00:24,904
Our example got a raise every year. Over four years, take-home went from $3,200 a month to $4,400. $1,200 more, every month.

6
00:00:25,324 --> 00:00:26,340
Here's where it went.

7
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:28,632
A nicer apartment: $300 more.

8
00:00:28,792 --> 00:00:30,726
A newer car payment: $250 more.

9
00:00:30,886 --> 00:00:33,129
Takeout and delivery: $200 more.

10
00:00:33,289 --> 00:00:35,021
Subscriptions and apps: $60.

11
00:00:35,181 --> 00:00:37,683
And everything just a bit nicer: $390.

12
00:00:38,103 --> 00:00:42,449
Savings from four years of raises? Zero. Same stress. Nicer stuff.

13
00:00:42,869 --> 00:00:51,190
And here's the catch. Spending ratchets up easily, but it almost never comes back down. A new lease, or a car payment, locks it in for years.

14
00:00:51,960 --> 00:00:53,658
The fix is the half-raise rule.

15
00:00:54,078 --> 00:01:00,414
Every time you get a raise, half goes to your life, and half goes straight to savings or debt, before you ever see it.

16
00:01:00,834 --> 00:01:09,131
So each $300 raise means $150 more to enjoy, and $150 saved automatically. You still feel every raise.

17
00:01:09,551 --> 00:01:14,930
Year one: $150 a month. Year two: $300. By year four: $600 a month.

18
00:01:15,350 --> 00:01:21,888
After four years at 4%, that's about $19,096. From money you never got used to spending.

19
00:01:22,658 --> 00:01:24,897
Already crept? Do a creep audit this week.

20
00:01:25,317 --> 00:01:26,025
Here's how.

21
00:01:26,185 --> 00:01:28,036
Pull a statement from three years ago.

22
00:01:28,196 --> 00:01:30,435
Compare it to last month, line by line.

23
00:01:30,595 --> 00:01:32,822
Mark every increase. Worth it, or not?

24
00:01:32,982 --> 00:01:37,255
Then cut one that's not worth it, and send that money to savings automatically.

25
00:01:38,025 --> 00:01:44,121
The Paycheck Planner in the description shows exactly where each paycheck goes, so creep has nowhere to hide.

26
00:01:44,541 --> 00:01:49,210
Subscribe. Next time: balance transfer cards, and when they're actually worth it.
